INT. PARKING GARAGE, LEVEL 3 - EARLY MORNING

Concrete. Oil stains. Orange cones set in a tight oval. A roller derby track, taped on the floor.

IRINA VASK, 20s, helmet under one arm, laces her skates on the hood of a dead Buick. COACH BOONE, 50s, whistle, bad knee, leans on a pillar with a clipboard and a paper cup of coffee.

Boone clicks his pen. Irina stands, rolls a slow lap past him, scuffs a wheel on a crack in the slab, curses under her breath, circles back, and Boone sips the coffee, checks the clipboard, and nods at nothing as she drifts to a stop at the cones.

IRINA
You're early, Coach.

BOONE
You're earlier, Irina.

IRINA
Couldn't sleep.

BOONE
Nobody sleeps before a bout.

She pulls on the helmet. He watches the ceiling. A pigeon shifts on a pipe. Somewhere below, a car door slams, an engine starts, and Boone and Irina both pretend not to hear it echo before she kicks off the pillar and takes a second lap, easy, unhurried, while he flips a page.

IRINA (CONT'D)
Count my laps, Coach.

BOONE
Already counting, kid.
